    "response": " To find the area between the curves \\( y = 2x \\) and \\( y = x^2 \\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the points of intersection of the two curves.**\n   Set \\( y = 2x \\) equal to \\( y = x^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   x^2 = 2x\n   \\]\n   Rearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 2x = 0\n   \\]\n   Factor out \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   x(x - 2) = 0\n   \\]\n   So, the solutions are:\n   \\[\n   x = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 2\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the points of intersection are \\( (0, 0) \\) and \\( (2, 4) \\).\n\n2. **Set up the integral to find the area between the curves.**\n   The area \\( A \\) between two curves \\( y = f(x) \\) and \\( y = g(x) \\) from \\( x = a \\) to \\( x = b \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   A = \\int_a^b [f(x) - g(x)] \\, dx\n   \\]\n   Here, \\( f(x) = 2x \\) and \\( g(x) = x^2 \\), and the interval of integration is from \\( x = 0 \\) to \\( x = 2 \\). So, we have:\n   \\[\n   A = \\int_0^2 [2x - x^2] \\, dx\n   \\]\n\n3. **Evaluate the integral.**\n   First, find the antiderivative of \\( 2x - x^2 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\int (2x - x^2) \\, dx = x^2 - \\frac{x^3}{3} + C\n   \\]\n   Now, evaluate this antiderivative from \\( x = 0 \\) to \\( x = 2 \\):\n   \\[\n   A = \\left[ x^2 - \\frac{x^3}{3} \\right]_0^2\n   \\]\n   Substitute the limits of integration:\n   \\[\n   A = \\left( 2^2 - \\frac{2^3}{3} \\right) - \\left( 0^2 - \\frac{0^3}{3} \\right)\n   \\]\n   Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   A = \\left( 4 - \\frac{8}{3} \\right) - 0\n   \\]\n   Convert 4 to a fraction with a denominator of 3:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{12}{3} - \\frac{8}{3}\n   \\]\n   Subtract the fractions:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{4}{3}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the region enclosed between the curves is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{4}{3}}\\).",
